Primary Dysmenorrhea
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Recurrent severe cramping with menses, Pain peaking within 24 hours of onset, Associated nausea/diarrhoea, Normal transvaginal pelvic ultrasound

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-040
Reason for Visit: Severe, incapacitating lower abdominal cramping, intractable vomiting, and acute distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a young female patient experiencing severe, unrelenting lower abdominal pain accompanied by repeated vomiting, causing an inability to maintain oral hydration. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively perform an abdominal examination, definitively exclude acute appendicitis, ovarian torsion, or an ectopic pregnancy, and to provide immediate parenteral anti-emetic and analgesic therapy prior to the next available daytime consultation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The cramps are so agonizing I can't stand up, and I keep throwing up every time I try to take a painkiller."
History of Present Illness: A 19-year-old female presents with an acute 12-hour episode of recurrent, severe, and cramping lower pelvic pain directly associated with the onset of her menstruation earlier today. The pain typically peaks at 24 hours. The severe cramping radiates to her lower back and anterior thighs. Today, the pain is accompanied by severe nausea, recurrent vomiting, and acute diarrhoea. The patient notes a complete absence of severe pelvic pain outside of this discrete menstrual window. The patient is unable to keep oral NSAIDs down due to the vomiting.
Functional Status: Curled in a fetal position, unable to walk due to severe cramping and dizziness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Impending dehydration due to repeated vomiting; parent is highly distressed by the severity of the pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/75 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 62 Kg
General: Pale, diaphoretic, groaning in pain, holding the lower abdomen.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals diffuse, severe tenderness strictly localized to the suprapubic and bilateral lower pelvic regions. Complete and absolute absence of generalized peritonism, focal right lower quadrant guarding, or rigidity (negative McBurney's and Rovsing's signs). Bowel sounds are hyperactive. Normal, non-tender renal angles. Complete absence of palpable abdominal masses. Documented history of an entirely normal transvaginal pelvic ultrasound last year demonstrating the absolute absence of endometriosis or ovarian cysts. Urine pregnancy test (beta-hCG) performed on-site is strictly negative.
Environmental Observations: Emesis basin by the bed containing small amounts of non-bilious fluid.
Medication Reconciliation: Attempted oral ibuprofen and paracetamol, immediately vomited.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Primary Dysmenorrhea - Status: Severe acute prostaglandin-mediated flare
Differentials:

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out by the specific menstrual timing, lack of fever, and completely soft abdomen lacking focal RLQ peritonism or guarding.

Ectopic Pregnancy/Ovarian Torsion - Ruled out definitively by the strictly negative point-of-care pregnancy test, bilateral rather than sudden sharp unilateral pain, and historically normal pelvic ultrasound.

Pelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID) - Ruled out due to absence of fever, absence of purulent vaginal discharge, and classic cyclical timing of the pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours home visit due to agonizing, incapacitating lower abdominal pain accompanied by intractable vomiting, a presentation that strongly mimics a severe acute surgical abdomen (e.g., appendicitis or torsion). An immediate, hands-on clinical examination and a point-of-care pregnancy test were absolutely mandatory to definitively rule out life-threatening surgical or obstetric emergencies. By confirming a soft, non-peritonitic abdomen and a negative pregnancy test, the physician safely diagnosed a severe, benign prostaglandin flare. The prompt administration of parenteral medication successfully aborted the pain and vomiting cycle, preventing a highly distressed and unnecessary emergency department transfer, strictly fulfilling the criteria for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered IM Ketorolac 30mg to provide potent, non-oral systemic prostaglandin inhibition, and IM Ondansetron 4mg stat to arrest the vomiting.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instead of waiting for the pain to become severe, begin taking oral NSAIDs (like Ibuprofen) rigidly every 8 hours starting exactly 24 hours before you expect your period to begin. By proactively blocking the chemical signals (prostaglandins) before they are fully released, you prevent the severe cramping and vomiting cycle from ever establishing, rewarding you with a manageable, functional menstrual cycle.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Heating pad.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 2 weeks to discuss starting continuous combined oral contraceptives for definitive hormonal suppression of symptoms.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if developing a high fever, sudden sharp unremitting unilateral (one-sided) pelvic pain, severe right lower quadrant pain, or vomiting of bright red blood.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis relies on the negative pregnancy test and soft abdomen today; if the pain changes character or becomes completely constant, immediate surgical review is required.
Clinical Course: Primary dysmenorrhea is caused by an overproduction of natural chemicals that force the uterus to cramp and cause vomiting. It is intensely painful but structurally completely healthy. Hormonal or preventative anti-inflammatory management is highly effective.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you develop a high fever, if the pain suddenly shifts to only one side and becomes sharp and constant, or if your stomach becomes hard to the touch.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your body is releasing too many chemicals called prostaglandins, which cause your uterus to spasm violently and directly trigger your brain to vomit. Because you are vomiting, the pills you took couldn't work. The injection bypasses your stomach to instantly block those chemicals and stop the spasms.
Actionable Steps: Apply a hot wheat bag directly to your lower pelvis right now. The intense local heat physically increases blood flow to the cramping uterine muscle, helping to flush away the excess pain chemicals and actively relaxing the severe spasm.

SMS INFORMATION

